   "date": "2016-08-27T11:38:05Z",
   "subject": "Standardised estimates in a CFA",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Does anyone have a reference for what the standardised estimates should be between observed and latent variables when conducting a CFA?<div><br></div><div>And if they fall below the recommended value would you remove that item or would you see if the other model fit indices are acceptable (e.g. RMSEA, CFI, TLI etc)?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks</div><div>Emma<br><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "iDpLHQ4GBAAJ",
   "from": "Oleksii Shestakovskyi",
   "author": "cc27ff6c8beac91f",
   "date": "2016-08-28T08:00:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: Standardised estimates in a CFA",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Emma,<div><br></div><div>I suspect you mean standardized estimates of factor loadings.</div><div><br></div><div>In general, there is no a strict inviolabe value, it may vary from field to filed and according to you theoretical assumptions and hypotheses.</div><div><br></div><div>The most frequent rule of tumb that I&#39;ve met is that its absolute value should not fall down beyond 0.4, as in EFA, although I saw .2, .3, .7 as well. </div><div><br></div><div>There</div><div><br></div><div>Whether to drop respectime item or not depends on you and your problem. If you only do a measurement model, and you have enough itmes left you may want to drop it. If it is a secondary loading, and &#39;primary&#39; loading from another factor is strong enough I&#39;d leave it.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Oleksii Shestakovskyi,</div><div>independent researcher, Ukraine</div><div><br><br>субота, 27 серпня 2016 р. 14:38:05 UTC+3 користувач Emma Mills написав:</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Does anyone have a reference for what the standardised estimates should be between observed and latent variables when conducting a CFA?<div><br></div><div>And if they fall below the recommended value would you remove that item or would you see if the other model fit indices are acceptable (e.g. RMSEA, CFI, TLI etc)?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks</div><div>Emma<br><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
