     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:11.25pt;word-break:break-all;background-image:initial;background-size:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:blue\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Sunday, 28 August 2016 14:58:33 UTC+1, Emma Mills  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Thank you, really appreciate your response!</div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 28 August 2016 at 09:00, Oleksii Shestakovskyi <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href rel=\"nofollow\" data-email-masked>o.s...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Emma,<div><br></div><div>I suspect you mean standardized estimates of factor loadings.</div><div><br></div><div>In general, there is no a strict inviolabe value, it may vary from field to filed and according to you theoretical assumptions and hypotheses.</div><div><br></div><div>The most frequent rule of tumb that I&#39;ve met is that its absolute value should not fall down beyond 0.4, as in EFA, although I saw .2, .3, .7 as well. </div><div><br></div><div>There</div><div><br></div><div>Whether to drop respectime item or not depends on you and your problem. If you only do a measurement model, and you have enough itmes left you may want to drop it. If it is a secondary loading, and &#39;primary&#39; loading from another factor is strong enough I&#39;d leave it.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Oleksii Shestakovskyi,</div><div>independent researcher, Ukraine</div><div><br><br>субота, 27 серпня 2016 р. 14:38:05 UTC+3 користувач Emma Mills написав:<div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Does anyone have a reference for what the standardised estimates should be between observed and latent variables when conducting a CFA?<div><br></div><div>And if they fall below the recommended value would you remove that item or would you see if the other model fit indices are acceptable (e.g. RMSEA, CFI, TLI etc)?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks</div><div>Emma<br><div><br></div><div><br></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div><div><div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to a topic in the Google Groups &quot;lavaan&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this topic, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/lavaan/tylrV8845Qg/unsubscribe\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/topic/lavaan/tylrV8845Qg/unsubscribe&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428017709000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2xL9G7PsXMh2sfti3hLOtr\">https://groups.google.com/d/topic/lavaan/tylrV8845Qg/unsubscribe</a>.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and all its topics, send an email to <a href rel=\"nofollow\" data-email-masked>lavaan+unsubscribe@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nTo post to this group, send email to <a href rel=\"nofollow\" data-email-masked>lav...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nVisit this group at <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428017709000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3pkcUcJB6c5fwnOdvOdtPw\">https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=17924280177090