     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Yes, you typically want to read the Std.all column, because it standardizes the coefficients in terms of both the predictors (latent common factors) and outcomes (indicators).  So standardized regressions indicate how many <i>SD</i>s the outcome changes when the predictor increases by 1 <i>SD</i>.  When the outcome only has 1 predictor, then the standardized slope is the same as the Pearson product-moment correlation coefficient (typically depicted <i>r</i>), but if there are multiple predictors of the outcome, then the standardized slope is a semipartial (or &quot;part&quot;) correlation.  <div><br></div><div>Standardized covariances are correlations.  If the variable is endogenous (predicted by something else in the model), then it is the correlation with that variable&#39;s residuals (i.e., with the predictor &quot;partialed out&quot;).  If both the variables have the same predictor, then their residual correlation is equivalent to a partial correlation (i.e., the correlation after the predictor is partialed out of both outcomes).</div><div><br></div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Postdoctoral Researcher, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div>UvA web page: <a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792428017711000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1NbNM8ss6aHbYRMbEXovBo\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
